 of roughly eight points toward Democrats and a long record of Democratic presidential wins. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party carries an incumbency advantage, and the race is fully open on both sides.\n\nNo general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available for this race yet, so the outlook rests on the state's fundamentals. One caution sign for Democrats: a September 2026 poll showed Republican nominee Drazan with a slight lead in Oregon's governor's race, hinting the state can run tighter than its partisan lean in a given cycle. Even so, the fundamentals point to a Democratic edge, with meaningful uncertainty about how the 2028 national environment will land in Oregon.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8229, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7795, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7825, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7642, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7754, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7778, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7766, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7748, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7749, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7747, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7742, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.749, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.7416, "margin": 7.0}]}